    title: "Reading Passage",
    level: "C1",
    text: "Facial expressions are the most immediate and universal form of nonverbal communication. A smile can convey warmth, politeness, amusement, or even discomfort — context determines the meaning. A genuine smile, known as a Duchenne smile, engages both the mouth and the eyes, creating crow's feet at the outer corners. A forced or polite smile only moves the lips. A frown — the downward pull of the eyebrows and mouth corners — signals displeasure, concentration, or confusion. Raised eyebrows indicate surprise, scepticism, or an invitation to continue speaking. A furrowed brow shows deep thought, worry, or frustration. Eye contact varies culturally: in Western business settings, steady eye contact shows confidence and honesty, while avoiding eye contact may suggest shyness, dishonesty, or — in many Asian and Middle Eastern cultures — respect for authority. A smirk, the one-sided half-smile, often communicates smugness, sarcasm, or quiet amusement. Pursed lips suggest disapproval or careful thought. A clenched jaw reveals tension, anger, or determination. Blushing — the involuntary reddening of the cheeks — signals embarrassment, shame, or sometimes attraction. Tears can express sadness, joy, frustration, or relief. Yawning, while physiological, is often read as boredom or fatigue. Understanding these expressions helps language learners decode unspoken messages and respond appropriately in conversation.",
